-- resources.lth: what an algorithm spends, as a typed record with its two compositions; the modulus
-- that says what an algorithm needs for a bound, partial (enough, infeasible with its reason, or
-- nothing known); and the scaling law that compares what was spent across bounds with the rate the
-- algorithm's theory states.
--
-- Written 2026-10-26 by Claude Opus 5.5 (agent C2), bite C2 of record/plan/audit-algebras-2026-10-22/
-- execution.md, on record/plan/after-core-2026-10-23/prompt-night-2026-10-26.md. Sources: the audit of
-- 2026-10-22 (record/plan/audit-algebras-2026-10-22/report.md, 5b1a05e), 4.4 (resources need several
-- compositions) and 6 (a modulus with a domain and an infeasible case; measured, sufficient and
-- extrapolated distinguished); D-2026-10-18-i with the coordinator's correction of 2026-10-23 (resources
-- a typed vector: work, span, peak memory; Blumofe and Leiserson, "Scheduling multithreaded
-- computations by work stealing", 1994); D-2026-10-18-d and L9.17 (the modulus of Bishop's
-- constructive analysis, 1967; tested by measuring the resource at several bounds, the slope in log
-- scale part of the law); D-2026-10-16-m (the step bound derived from the theory, never a constant);
-- L13.5 (the floor below which no budget suffices); L6.2. What is mine is marked [proposed].
--
-- Index:
--   §1 Resources: work, span, peak memory; sequence and parallel, each part a monoid image.   done
--   §2 The modulus: a bound and the problem's features to what suffices, or why nothing
--      does, or nothing known; its laws (the audit's list).                                done
--   §3 Scaling: the least-squares slope of the log of what was spent on the log of the
--      stated rate, within the bound the spread of a count allows.                         done
-- The model of §1 is resources_model.lut's. No planner reads these yet (L6.3); the first consumer is
-- examples/bisection.lut.

import "numbers.lth" Elementary Doubles abs
import "laws.lth" Law Within Case

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 Resources

-- The resources of a run (the coordinator's correction to D-2026-10-18-i): the work, every step
-- counted; the span, the longest chain of steps that wait on each other (the critical path); the peak
-- memory, the most held at once. Counts, so `Nat`: a sum of counts is exact and so associative, which
-- a sum of doubles is not. [proposed] The units are the algorithm's (evaluations of a function, values
-- held); a model says which.
Resources = { work : Nat, span : Nat, memory : Nat }

-- What composes resources: nothing spent, one run after another, two runs side by side.
ResourceAlgebra : { none : Resources, sequence : Resources -> Resources -> Resources, parallel : Resources -> Resources -> Resources }

-- the parts, as functions, and the two operations of the counts the parts compose by
workOf (r : Resources) : Nat := r.work
spanOf (r : Resources) : Nat := r.span
memoryOf (r : Resources) : Nat := r.memory
addCounts (a b : Nat) : Nat := a + b
largerCount (a b : Nat) : Nat := if a <= b then b else a

-- The laws: each composition a commutative monoid with nothing spent as its neutral, and each part
-- carried to the counts by its own operation (`Preserving`, the core's homomorphism): work adds in
-- sequence and in parallel; the span adds in sequence and takes the larger in parallel (the two
-- branches wait on neither); the peak memory takes the larger in sequence (the first run's memory is
-- free before the second's starts) and adds in parallel (both are held at once). No one semiring
-- holds the three (the audit's 4.4: `max` with neutral 0 and `+` has no absorbing zero); the record is
-- the product of six monoid images, typed by part.
ResourceAlgebra (m : ResourceAlgebra) :=
  Associative Resources (structural Resources) m.sequence & Commutative Resources Resources (structural Resources) m.sequence
  & Neutral Resources (structural Resources) m.sequence m.none
  & Associative Resources (structural Resources) m.parallel & Commutative Resources Resources (structural Resources) m.parallel
  & Neutral Resources (structural Resources) m.parallel m.none
  & Preserving Resources Nat (structural Nat) m.sequence addCounts workOf
  & Preserving Resources Nat (structural Nat) m.parallel addCounts workOf
  & Preserving Resources Nat (structural Nat) m.sequence addCounts spanOf
  & Preserving Resources Nat (structural Nat) m.parallel largerCount spanOf
  & Preserving Resources Nat (structural Nat) m.sequence largerCount memoryOf
  & Preserving Resources Nat (structural Nat) m.parallel addCounts memoryOf

-- the model, `workSpanMemory`, is resources_model.lut's

-- what is spent is no more than what is spent elsewhere, part by part (the order a sufficient amount
-- is compared in)
resourcesBelow (r s : Resources) : Bool := r.work <= s.work and r.span <= s.span and r.memory <= s.memory

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 The modulus

-- A bound demanded: a positive distance (the tolerance of a root, of a fixed point, of an
-- approximation), in the units of the law it bounds.
Bound : U := { b : Float64 | 0.0 < b }

-- Why no amount suffices: the bound is below the floor the rounding sets (L13.5), or the problem is
-- outside the domain where the algorithm converges.
Infeasibility = { belowFloor : Unit || outsideDomain : Unit }
-- What a modulus says of a bound at a problem (the audit's 6: "a partial or extended-valued modulus,
-- returning a bound, an infeasibility reason or insufficient knowledge"): the resources that suffice,
-- infeasible with the reason, or nothing known. [proposed] The audit also distinguishes an
-- extrapolated estimate from a sufficient bound; no constructor for it until the planner extrapolates
-- (P8): what a run measured is the tester's record (tester.py's scaling record), what suffices is
-- `sufficient`, and nothing else is claimed.
Need = { sufficient : Resources || infeasible : Infeasibility || unknown : Unit }

isSufficient (n : Need) : Bool := match n { sufficient r -> true ; _ -> false }
isInfeasible (n : Need) : Bool := match n { infeasible i -> true ; _ -> false }
-- the order of what is needed: less is cheaper; a known sufficient amount is below nothing known,
-- which is below infeasible (a request whose looser form is infeasible is infeasible too, and one
-- whose stronger form is known to suffice is known at the looser)
needBelow (a b : Need) : Bool := match a {
  sufficient r -> (match b { sufficient s -> resourcesBelow r s ; _ -> true }) ;
  unknown u -> (match b { sufficient s -> false ; _ -> true }) ;
  infeasible i -> (match b { infeasible j -> true ; _ -> false }) }

-- A modulus over problems described by their features `F` (size, condition, the width of a bracket,
-- the scale of the values): what a bound needs, the floor below which nothing suffices, and the domain
-- where the algorithm converges.
Modulus (F : U) : { need : Bound -> F -> Need, floor : F -> Float64, domain : F -> Bool }

-- the audit's 6, "Missing laws": a stronger bound never needs less (the order above, at two bounds of
-- one problem)
StrongerNeedsNoLess (F : U) (m : Modulus F) (f : F) (b c : Bound) : Bool :=
  if b <= c then needBelow (m.need c f) (m.need b f) else true
-- below the floor, infeasible (L13.5: the rounding's resolution, which no budget passes)
BelowTheFloorIsInfeasible (F : U) (m : Modulus F) (f : F) (b : Bound) : Bool :=
  if b < m.floor f then isInfeasible (m.need b f) else true
-- outside the convergence domain, no amount is promised
OutsideTheDomainPromisesNothing (F : U) (m : Modulus F) (f : F) (b : Bound) : Bool :=
  if m.domain f then true else not (isSufficient (m.need b f))
-- [proposed] inside its domain and at or above its floor, the modulus promises an amount: the domain is
-- where it is a modulus, so a modulus states it rather than answering `unknown` there
InsideTheDomainSuffices (F : U) (m : Modulus F) (f : F) (b : Bound) : Bool :=
  if m.domain f and m.floor f <= b then isSufficient (m.need b f) else true
Modulus (F : U) (m : Modulus F) := StrongerNeedsNoLess F m & BelowTheFloorIsInfeasible F m
  & OutsideTheDomainPromisesNothing F m & InsideTheDomainSuffices F m

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 Scaling (L9.17)

-- What an algorithm's theory states of how a resource grows as the bound tightens: the resource is a
-- constant times `rate b f` to the `exponent`, counted in `grain`s (a step, a sample), so that the
-- count is at least the scaled rate and less than one grain above it. Bisection: `log2 (L / b)`,
-- exponent one, grain one step (examples/bisection.lut); a sequential test: `log (1 / alpha) / b^2`,
-- exponent one, grain one sample.
Scaling (F : U) : { rate : Bound -> F -> Float64, exponent : Float64, grain : Float64 }

sumOf (xs : List Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ; cons x rest -> x + sumOf rest }
countOf (xs : List Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ; cons x rest -> 1.0 + countOf rest }
meanOf (xs : List Float64) : Float64 := sumOf xs / countOf xs
leastOf (xs : List Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ; cons x rest -> (match rest { nil -> x ;
  cons y more -> (if x <= leastOf rest then x else leastOf rest) }) }
largestMagnitude (xs : List Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ; cons x rest -> (if abs x <= largestMagnitude rest then largestMagnitude rest else abs x) }
logs (e : Elementary) (xs : List Float64) : List Float64 := match xs { nil -> nil ; cons x rest -> cons (e.log x) (logs e rest) }
-- the sums of the least-squares fit of `ys` on `xs`, the lists paired in order: the cross products and
-- the squares of the deviations from the means, and the deviations' magnitudes
crossDeviations (xs ys : List Float64) (mx my : Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ;
  cons x rest -> (match ys { nil -> 0.0 ; cons y more -> (x - mx) * (y - my) + crossDeviations rest more mx my }) }
squaredDeviations (xs : List Float64) (mx : Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ; cons x rest -> (x - mx) * (x - mx) + squaredDeviations rest mx }
absoluteDeviations (xs : List Float64) (mx : Float64) : Float64 := match xs { nil -> 0.0 ; cons x rest -> abs (x - mx) + absoluteDeviations rest mx }
slopeOf (xs ys : List Float64) : Float64 :=
  crossDeviations xs ys (meanOf xs) (meanOf ys) / squaredDeviations xs (meanOf xs)

-- The scaling law: the slope of `log spent` on `log rate` is the stated exponent, within the bound
-- the count's grain allows. Write `y = log spent`, `x = log rate`, `p` the exponent: by the statement,
-- `y_i = log c + p x_i + e_i` with `e_i = log (spent_i / (c rate_i^p))` in `[0, log (n / (n - g))]`,
-- `n` the least count spent and `g` the grain. The fitted slope is `p + sum (x_i - mx)(e_i - me) / Sxx`,
-- and `|e_i - me|` is at most the width of that interval, so the slope is within
-- `E sum |x_i - mx| / Sxx` of `p`, `E = log (n / (n - g))`, `Sxx` the sum of squared deviations of the
-- `x`. The logarithms and the sums round: a perturbation `dy` of the `y` moves the slope by at most
-- `max |dy| sum |x_i - mx| / Sxx` by the same argument, and one of the `x` by about `|p|` times that;
-- with each logarithm within `k` units in the last place (`Elementary.ulps`) and each subtraction and
-- product one rounding more, `4 k epsilon (1 + max |y| + |p| max |x|)` bounds both [proposed: a
-- first-order room, many orders below `E` at any count below 2^40]. The law states its range: a
-- grain below the least count, and two rates at least that differ (`Sxx` positive); outside it,
-- nothing is claimed. Wrong exponents are what it refutes (the control states 2 for bisection's 1); a
-- wrong constant it cannot see, the slope being blind to `c`: that is the modulus's law,
-- `NeedsNoMoreThanItsModulus` at the algorithm.
ScalesAs (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (exponent grain : Float64) (spent rates : List Float64) : Law :=
  { xs := logs e rates
    ys := logs e spent
    least := leastOf spent
    sxx := squaredDeviations xs (meanOf xs)
    if grain < least and 0.0 < sxx then
      Within Float64 (fn a b { abs (a - b) })
        ((e.log (least / (least - grain)) + 4.0 * e.ulps * d.epsilon * (1.0 + largestMagnitude ys + abs exponent * largestMagnitude xs))
          * absoluteDeviations xs (meanOf xs) / sxx)
        (slopeOf xs ys) exponent
    else Case true }
